          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If someone has a raised blood pressure reading but no diagnosis of hypertension, which chapter does that belong in?",
          "answer": "A raised blood pressure reading, without a diagnosis of hypertension, belongs in Chapter 18 of the ICD-10-CM classification. This chapter covers symptoms, signs, and abnormal findings. An elevated blood pressure reading in someone without a hypertension diagnosis is considered a finding rather than a disease. The relevant codes for this would be found in the R00 to R09 range, which covers symptoms and signs related to the circulatory and respiratory systems.\n\nThis classification is based on the documentation of a diagnosis; without a formal diagnosis of hypertension, even multiple elevated readings are classified under abnormal findings in Chapter 18. This information comes from the documents \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\" and \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings (R00-R99)\".\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
